From 013f38968132a20cae719aeecd252e0d1b52f012 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Green <dgreen2001personal@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 19:55:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] video_calls: Add authenticated Jitsi calls scoped to a
 conversation.

Zulip generates Jitsi room names in the browser with Math.random and
does not check who is joining; anyone with the link is in. That is
adequate for meet.jit.si, but a self-hosted deployment has no way to
express "only the people in this conversation".

Add POST /calls/jitsi/create, following the pattern of the existing
server-brokered providers, with one difference: it performs an
authorization check. Membership of the conversation is what entitles a
user to a call, so the endpoint requires a channel subscription, or that
the direct message recipients are users this user may reach. The four
existing call endpoints check nothing, which is harmless only because
the rooms they hand out are unauthenticated anyway.

Room names are derived by HMAC over the conversation and a rotatable
epoch rather than randomly generated, because a room name is functionally
a secret once it can be pasted into a chat, and because rotation gives a
recovery path when one leaks. The epoch is signed and bound to its
conversation, following the approach get_bigbluebutton_url already uses
for its moderator flag, so that it can round-trip through an untrusted
client without becoming a way to reach into someone else's conversation.

Tokens are minted for a single room and expire in two minutes. Prosody
validates a token when the client connects and again at MUC join and
never afterwards, so a short lifetime costs a user nothing and bounds how
long a leaked token remains useful for joining. It does nothing about an
already established session; there is no revocation in this model.

The behavior is unchanged for servers that have not configured JITSI_JWT_*
settings.

Fixes part of #28657.

